<!-- ======================================================= A -->
<section class="page">
  <h2>A. The problem, measured in the public data <span class="tag built">BUILT + TESTED</span></h2>
  <p>The challenge states that long-term price data for Assam are incomplete. The public Agmarknet record confirms it, and shows exactly how: whole years are missing, the number of reporting markets swings widely, and markets change names.</p>
  <img class="fig" src="${figdir}f1_reporting_timeline.png" alt="Weekly number of Assam markets reporting prices">
  <div class="grid2" style="margin-top:3mm">
    <div>
      <table>
        <tr><th>Measure (Assam, public Agmarknet record)</th><th class="n">Value</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Price records, ${assam_first} – ${assam_last}</td><td class="n">${assam_rows}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Weeks with no Assam price at all</td><td class="n">${weeks_empty} of ${weeks_total}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Longest gap</td><td class="n">${longest_gap}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Median markets reporting in a week</td><td class="n">${median_markets_week}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Raw market names → actual markets after harmonising</td><td class="n">${assam_markets_raw} → ${assam_markets_harm}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Market × commodity series reported in last 156 weeks</td><td class="n">${n_pairs}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>… median share of weeks with a price</td><td class="n">${median_cov}%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>… series with a price in at least half of weeks</td><td class="n">${n_ge50}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Weekly prices flagged as implausible (&gt;3× jump)</td><td class="n">${outlier_pct}%</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Renamed markets break time series</h3>
      <table>
        <tr><th>Market</th><th>Names in the record (first → last use)</th></tr>
        ${renames_rows}
      </table>
      <p class="small">${assam_markets_renamed} markets appear under two or three names. A naive analysis would treat each name as a separate, short series. The prototype maps them with a documented rule plus a short alias table.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="small"><b>Price dispersion.</b> On a typical day, the highest-priced Assam market reports ${disp_sentence} more than the lowest for the same commodity (median over days with at least three markets reporting; part of the gap reflects quality and variety). A state average hides choices that matter to farmers.</p>
</section>

<section class="page">
  <h2>A (cont.) Coverage and choice of pilot series <span class="tag built">BUILT + TESTED</span></h2>
  <img class="fig" src="${figdir}f2_coverage_heatmap.png" alt="Coverage heatmap">
  <p style="margin-top:2mm"><b>Selection rule, fixed before any modelling:</b> the three Assam market × commodity series with the most weeks reported in the last 156 complete weeks (${win_start} to ${win_end}), whatever they are. Result: <b>${sel_market}</b> (${sel_district} district, Guwahati's main wholesale vegetable market) for <b>${sel_commodities}</b>, each reported in ${sel_weeks} of 156 weeks (${sel_cov}%). Even the best-covered Assam series miss a third of weeks, including a three-month gap when Agmarknet moved to its new platform.</p>
  <p>All three are perishable or semi-perishable vegetables, so the prototype focuses on short-horizon timing. Storable crops such as potato, paddy or mustard will be added in the pilot, as agreed with the department, using the same pipeline.</p>
  <h3>Comparable markets used for learning</h3>
  <p>For the three commodities, the prototype also uses every market reported in ${comp_states}: ${comp_rows} records, mostly from 2016 onward. ${comp_note}</p>
  ${data_summary_table}
</section>

