<!-- ======================================================= D -->
<section class="page">
  <h2>D. How the prototype works <span class="tag built">BUILT + TESTED</span></h2>
  <div class="arch" style="grid-template-columns: repeat(5, 1fr)">
    <div class="c"><b>1 · Fetch</b>data.gov.in OGD API; every page cached with query and retrieval time (<code>src/fetch.py</code>).</div>
    <div class="c"><b>2 · Clean</b>Harmonise market names; weekly median prices; flag &gt;3× jumps against the trailing median; keep gaps explicit (<code>apa/clean.py</code>).</div>
    <div class="c"><b>3 · Features</b>Own price changes (1–52 weeks), volatility, reporting completeness, Assam and neighbour-State price indices, price vs peers, season (<code>apa/features.py</code>).</div>
    <div class="c"><b>4 · Model</b>One global gradient-boosted quantile model per horizon (P10, P50, P90), scikit-learn, native handling of missing values (<code>apa/model.py</code>).</div>
    <div class="c"><b>5 · Decide</b>Hold / Sell / Store rule from band, losses and shelf life; seasonal crop profiles (<code>apa/advisory.py</code>, <code>apa/seasonal.py</code>).</div>
  </div>
  <h3>Why a global model?</h3>
  <p>Fitting one model per market fails when each market has a short, gappy history. A global model is trained on all markets at once: it learns how prices of a commodity typically move after, say, a sharp rise or a regional fall, and applies that to every market, including ones with little history. This is the approach that performs well on large collections of short series in the forecasting literature (Montero-Manso &amp; Hyndman, 2021; M5 competition, 2022). Missing weeks are passed to the model as missing values, not filled in.</p>
  <h3>Evaluation protocol (no look-ahead)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Rolling origin:</b> test period ${test_start} to ${end_week} (12 months). Every 4 weeks the model is retrained using only examples whose outcome was already known; it then forecasts the next 4 weeks of origins.</li>
    <li><b>Features</b> at a forecast week use only data up to that week; regional indices are built from markets' past changes only.</li>
    <li><b>Benchmarks</b> on exactly the same forecasts: <i>no change</i> (next price = this week's) and <i>seasonal naive</i> (this year's change = the same weeks' change last year; falls back to no change when last year is missing, as in ${all_snaive_fallback_pct}% of cases).</li>
    <li><b>Measures:</b> mean absolute percentage error (MAPE) of the price; direction accuracy over three classes (down &gt;5%, flat, up &gt;5%); share of actual prices inside the P10–P90 band (target 80%).</li>
    <li><b>Scope:</b> all ${n_series_assam} Assam market series of the three commodities (${all_n} forecasts), with the three selected series reported separately.</li>
  </ul>
